From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: domicile CI <ci@domicile.invalid>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 18:40:54 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] domicile: the rest of the control protocol that survives the
 fork

Twenty-one of the protocol's twenty-nine members, taking the channel from a
vertical slice to something a shell could be written against.

The other eight are absent on purpose. place_portal, remove_portal,
declare_bands, render_band, claim_pointer and app_composited are the bands and
copy-path protocol, which docs/architecture/ENGINE-FORK.md lists under what the
fork scraps: layout positions the layer now and the page has stopped reporting
where its own boxes are. Freezing them into the browser would make removing a
dying protocol cost a release build.

Two shapes are decisions rather than convenience:

  - DomicileAppEvent carries size as genuinely optional. A toplevel maps before
    it draws, so an appeared window may not yet have said how big it wants to
    be; flattening absence to zero is what opens a window at nothing at all.
    Verified: app_appeared without a size arrives as hasSize:false, and one
    with a size arrives as 640x480.
  - modifiers has its own event. Four xkb values smuggled through fields named
    width and height reads as working and means nothing to whoever uses it.

Also fixes a defect found reviewing this code rather than running it: on giving
up after 30s of retries only the client remote was reset, which silences the
inbound direction and leaves the page holding a channel that looks alive and
swallows everything written to it. The channel now owns its receiver and closes
the page's end, which is the only signal a shell can observe.

Verified end to end against a stand-in compositor. Outbound, in wire format:
  hello, spawn, focus_app, focus_chrome, key, pointer_motion,
  resize_app {size:[800,600]}, set_desktop_size {size:[1920,1080]}
Inbound, as typed events:
  app_appeared (with and without a size), app_resized, app_titled, app_cursor,
  focus_changed, shortcut, app_closed
And both argument guards throw: focusApp("") and setDevicePixelRatio(0).

Adding a member costs a near-full Blink rebuild, because a new inbound message
needs a new event type and that invalidates the generated bindings. These
nineteen were added together for that reason.
